  <h3>4. Drawer / mixed stack syntax</h3>
  <p>The stack is always entered <strong>bottom → top</strong>.</p>
  <div class="help-warning"><strong>Important:</strong> <code>O</code> is the <strong>letter O ("oh")</strong>, short for <strong>Open</strong>. It is <strong>not the number zero (<code>0</code>)</strong>. If <code>O8</code> could be mistaken for <code>08</code>, type <code>Open 8</code> instead; both forms mean the same thing.</div>
  <table class="help-table">
    <thead><tr><th>Example</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><code>7, 7, 9.5</code></td><td>Legacy all-drawer mode: three drawer boxes because the entire list is bare numbers.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>D7, D7, D9.5</code></td><td>The same three drawer boxes, written explicitly.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>D9, D9, 15</code></td><td>Two 9-unit drawers followed by a 15-unit clear open cubby. Once D is used, bare numbers are open cubbies.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>D7, O9.5, D7</code></td><td>7-unit drawer, 9.5-unit clear open cubby, 7-unit drawer. The <code>O</code> is the letter O for Open, not zero.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>Open 8, D10</code></td><td>Bottom 8-unit open cubby uses the cabinet bottom and gets one top divider. <code>Open 8</code> and <code>O8</code> are equivalent.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>D10, O8</code></td><td>Top 8-unit open cubby gets one bottom divider and no added full top. Here <code>O</code> is the letter O.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p><strong>D = Drawer.</strong> <strong>O = Open</strong> and is the <strong>letter O (oh), never the digit 0 (zero)</strong>. <code>Open 8</code>, <code>Cubby 8</code>, and <code>O8</code> are accepted synonyms. For convenience, once a stack contains an explicit <code>D</code>, an unprefixed number also means an open cubby. A completely bare list remains all drawers for compatibility with older projects. Fixed cubby divider panels are pocket-screwed into the cabinet sides.</p>

  <h3>5. Open-shelf heights</h3>
  <p>For an Open shelf cabinet, use the same vertical-stack field to enter <strong>clear opening heights</strong>, bottom to top.</p>
  <pre>9.5, 12, auto</pre>
  <p>This creates three clear openings. The first two are fixed to the entered heights and the final/top opening uses the remaining height. Use <code>auto</code> only for the final opening. If the field is blank, the Shelves count is used and the shelves are spaced evenly.</p>
  <p>These shelves are still <strong>adjustable shelf-pin shelves</strong>; the entered heights describe the modeled layout, not fixed pocket-screw locations.</p>

  <h3>6. Construction model</h3>
  <p>CabinetDrafter currently models the following construction system:</p><div class="help-callout"><strong>Face frames in 1.1:</strong> select the front construction per cabinet. Global face-frame settings control stile width, rail width, frame thickness/material, overlay, and inset reveal. Frames are perimeter-only in 1.1; intermediate rails between individual drawers/cubbies are a future enhancement.</div>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Per-cabinet front construction:</strong> frameless, face-frame overlay, or face-frame inset.</li>
    <li><strong>Pocket-screw structural joinery.</strong></li>
    <li>Standard carcass: 3/4 plywood.</li>
    <li>Drawer boxes: 1/2 birch plywood sides, front, back, and bottom.</li>
    <li>Drawer bottom fits completely between the four drawer walls; no dado or rabbet.</li>
    <li>Drawer slide clearance: 1/2 per side / 1 total by default.</li>
